('setting-screen-time-limits-that-actually-work-without-the-overwhelm', 'Setting Screen-time Limits that Actually Work Without the Overwhelm', 'Setting Screen-time Limits that Actually Work is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore. What follows is the', 'Marcus Bell', '2024-01-15', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#settingscreentime','#worklifebalance','#lifehacks']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/technology,child,tablet?lock=1000', 'A photo related to setting screen-time limits that actually work.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"Setting Screen-time Limits that Actually Work is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore."},{"type":"p","text":"What follows is the approach we keep coming back to: simple enough to start this week, flexible enough to survive a bad Tuesday."},{"type":"h2","text":"Give the choice, keep the frame"},{"type":"p","text":"\"Bath now or after this show?\" beats \"Go take a bath.\" The child gets agency; you keep the outcome. It's the oldest trick in the parenting book because it works."},{"type":"h2","text":"Siblings: coach, don't referee"},{"type":"p","text":"Stepping in to declare a winner teaches kids to recruit you as a weapon. Narrating both sides and handing the problem back — \"you two figure out a fair turn\" — builds the skill that actually ends the fighting."},{"type":"h2","text":"Connection before correction"},{"type":"p","text":"Behavior is communication. A ten-second reconnect — eye level, a hand on the shoulder, a real question — resolves more than a raised voice ever will, and it costs almost nothing."},{"type":"h2","text":"Let them be bored"},{"type":"p","text":"Boredom is the doorway to imagination. Resisting the urge to fill every gap teaches kids to generate their own fun — a skill worth more than any enrichment class."},{"type":"h2","text":"Lower the bar on purpose"},{"type":"p","text":"Perfectionism is the enemy of a warm home. A \"good enough\" dinner eaten together beats a Pinterest meal served to a stressed-out table."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, write down the plan so it survives a chaotic day. Next, start smaller than feels necessary — momentum beats ambition. Then, agree on who owns it so it isn't quietly one person's job."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"Want setting screen-time limits that actually work to run itself? That's what Bubaly does — one calm home for your family's schedule, tasks, meals, and money, with AI that stays a step ahead. Start at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"Give it a couple of weeks before you judge it. Habits feel awkward right up until the moment they feel obvious."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('the-honest-guide-to-handling-toddler-tantrums-in-public', 'The Honest Guide to Handling Toddler Tantrums in Public', 'Handling Toddler Tantrums in Public is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore. What follows is the approach ', 'Priya Nair', '2024-02-05', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#handlingtoddler','#raisingkids','#parentinghacks']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/toddler,parent?lock=1001', 'A photo related to handling toddler tantrums in public.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"Handling Toddler Tantrums in Public is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore."},{"type":"p","text":"What follows is the approach we keep coming back to: simple enough to start this week, flexible enough to survive a bad Tuesday."},{"type":"h2","text":"Consistency over intensity"},{"type":"p","text":"A quiet rule kept every day outperforms a dramatic one kept once. Kids are scientists; they run experiments on our boundaries, and steadiness gives them the answer faster than volume."},{"type":"h2","text":"Connection before correction"},{"type":"p","text":"Behavior is communication. A ten-second reconnect — eye level, a hand on the shoulder, a real question — resolves more than a raised voice ever will, and it costs almost nothing."},{"type":"h2","text":"Repair is the real lesson"},{"type":"p","text":"You will lose your temper. The magic isn't never rupturing — it's the repair afterward. \"I was frustrated and I snapped; that wasn't fair\" teaches more about relationships than a hundred calm days."},{"type":"h2","text":"The 2-minute head start"},{"type":"p","text":"Transitions are where meltdowns live. A gentle countdown — \"two more minutes, then we clean up\" — respects a child's need to finish a thought and heads off half your battles."},{"type":"h2","text":"One-on-one beats the group"},{"type":"p","text":"Even a quick errand run solo with one child fills a cup that group time can't reach. Rotate who gets the passenger seat and watch the rivalry cool."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, start smaller than feels necessary — momentum beats ambition. Next, decide the one default that removes a daily decision. Then, give it two weeks before you judge whether it's working."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"This is exactly the kind of invisible work Bubaly was built for. Instead of holding handling toddler tantrums in public in your head, you hand it to your family's shared system — Bubaly turns plans into real calendar events, reminders, lists, and gentle nudges, so the whole household stays in sync. Explore it at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"Give it a couple of weeks before you judge it. Habits feel awkward right up until the moment they feel obvious."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('raising-a-confident-only-child-that-actually-sticks', 'Raising a Confident Only Child That Actually Sticks', 'Raising a Confident Only Child is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore. We''ll walk through what actually m', 'Sofia Alvarez', '2024-02-28', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#raisingconfident','#organizedhome','#lifehacks']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/child,parent,together?lock=1002', 'A photo related to raising a confident only child.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"Raising a Confident Only Child is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore."},{"type":"p","text":"We'll walk through what actually moves the needle, what to skip, and how to make it feel less like a chore and more like a shared routine."},{"type":"h2","text":"Let them be bored"},{"type":"p","text":"Boredom is the doorway to imagination. Resisting the urge to fill every gap teaches kids to generate their own fun — a skill worth more than any enrichment class."},{"type":"h2","text":"One-on-one beats the group"},{"type":"p","text":"Even a quick errand run solo with one child fills a cup that group time can't reach. Rotate who gets the passenger seat and watch the rivalry cool."},{"type":"h2","text":"Connection before correction"},{"type":"p","text":"Behavior is communication. A ten-second reconnect — eye level, a hand on the shoulder, a real question — resolves more than a raised voice ever will, and it costs almost nothing."},{"type":"h2","text":"Special time, protected"},{"type":"p","text":"Ten minutes of undivided, child-led attention a day does more for behavior than an hour of correction. Put it on the calendar so it survives busy weeks."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, write down the plan so it survives a chaotic day. Next, decide the one default that removes a daily decision. Then, put it somewhere the whole family can see it."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"If keeping raising a confident only child on track sounds exhausting, that's the point where Bubaly quietly helps. It's the AI operating system for family life — it remembers, reminds, and coordinates so you don't have to carry it all alone. See how at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"Give it a couple of weeks before you judge it. Habits feel awkward right up until the moment they feel obvious."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('sibling-rivalry-between-close-in-age-kids-a-calm-practical-guide', 'Sibling Rivalry Between Close-in-age Kids: A Calm, Practical Guide', 'There''s a version of sibling rivalry between close-in-age kids that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. Here''s the framework, the small habits that make it ', 'Elena Rossi', '2024-03-13', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#siblingrivalry','#organizedhome','#worklifebalance']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/children,friends,playing?lock=1003', 'A photo related to sibling rivalry between close-in-age kids.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"There's a version of sibling rivalry between close-in-age kids that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. This is about getting to the second one."},{"type":"p","text":"Here's the framework, the small habits that make it stick, and the honest caveats nobody mentions."},{"type":"h2","text":"Siblings: coach, don't referee"},{"type":"p","text":"Stepping in to declare a winner teaches kids to recruit you as a weapon. Narrating both sides and handing the problem back — \"you two figure out a fair turn\" — builds the skill that actually ends the fighting."},{"type":"h2","text":"Connection before correction"},{"type":"p","text":"Behavior is communication. A ten-second reconnect — eye level, a hand on the shoulder, a real question — resolves more than a raised voice ever will, and it costs almost nothing."},{"type":"h2","text":"One-on-one beats the group"},{"type":"p","text":"Even a quick errand run solo with one child fills a cup that group time can't reach. Rotate who gets the passenger seat and watch the rivalry cool."},{"type":"h2","text":"Lower the bar on purpose"},{"type":"p","text":"Perfectionism is the enemy of a warm home. A \"good enough\" dinner eaten together beats a Pinterest meal served to a stressed-out table."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, pick the single piece of sibling rivalry between close-in-age kids that causes the most friction this week. Next, decide the one default that removes a daily decision. Then, give it two weeks before you judge whether it's working."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"Bubaly was designed for moments exactly like this: it takes sibling rivalry between close-in-age kids off your mental to-do list and turns it into shared, automatic routines the whole family can see. Learn more at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"Start small, stay consistent, and let the system do the remembering so you don't have to."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('winning-at-getting-kids-to-listen-the-first-time-on-a-normal-week', 'Winning at Getting Kids to Listen the First Time on a Normal Week', 'The secret to getting kids to listen the first time isn''t more willpower. Here''s the framework, the small habits that make it stick, and the honest caveats nobody mentions.', 'Daniel Okafor', '2024-04-01', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#gettingkids','#lifehacks','#organizedhome']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/family,children,parenting?lock=1004', 'A photo related to getting kids to listen the first time.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"The secret to getting kids to listen the first time isn't more willpower. It's less friction — fewer decisions, clearer defaults, and a plan everyone can see."},{"type":"p","text":"Here's the framework, the small habits that make it stick, and the honest caveats nobody mentions."},{"type":"h2","text":"Repair is the real lesson"},{"type":"p","text":"You will lose your temper. The magic isn't never rupturing — it's the repair afterward. \"I was frustrated and I snapped; that wasn't fair\" teaches more about relationships than a hundred calm days."},{"type":"h2","text":"Name the feeling before the fix"},{"type":"p","text":"Kids (and adults) calm down when they feel understood, not when they're handed a solution. A quick \"you really wanted more time, huh?\" lands better than a lecture. With getting kids to listen the first time, naming the emotion first buys you the cooperation the logic needs."},{"type":"h2","text":"Routines beat reminders"},{"type":"p","text":"A predictable sequence — same steps, same order — means you stop being the nag and the routine becomes the boss. Kids resist people; they rarely resist a chart they helped make."},{"type":"h2","text":"Let them be bored"},{"type":"p","text":"Boredom is the doorway to imagination. Resisting the urge to fill every gap teaches kids to generate their own fun — a skill worth more than any enrichment class."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, agree on who owns it so it isn't quietly one person's job. Next, write down the plan so it survives a chaotic day. Then, pick the single piece of getting kids to listen the first time that causes the most friction this week."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"This is exactly the kind of invisible work Bubaly was built for. Instead of holding getting kids to listen the first time in your head, you hand it to your family's shared system — Bubaly turns plans into real calendar events, reminders, lists, and gentle nudges, so the whole household stays in sync. Explore it at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"You'll still have messy days. That's fine. A good routine bends without breaking."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('winning-at-positive-discipline-for-strong-willed-kids-on-a-normal-week', 'Winning at Positive Discipline for Strong-willed Kids on a Normal Week', 'There''s a version of positive discipline for strong-willed kids that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. What follows is the approach we keep coming back to', 'Sofia Alvarez', '2024-04-13', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#positivediscipline','#familyfirst','#dadlife']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/family,children,parenting?lock=1005', 'A photo related to positive discipline for strong-willed kids.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"There's a version of positive discipline for strong-willed kids that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. This is about getting to the second one."},{"type":"p","text":"What follows is the approach we keep coming back to: simple enough to start this week, flexible enough to survive a bad Tuesday."},{"type":"h2","text":"Name the feeling before the fix"},{"type":"p","text":"Kids (and adults) calm down when they feel understood, not when they're handed a solution. A quick \"you really wanted more time, huh?\" lands better than a lecture. With positive discipline for strong-willed kids, naming the emotion first buys you the cooperation the logic needs."},{"type":"h2","text":"Praise the effort, not the trait"},{"type":"p","text":"\"You worked hard on that\" grows a child who tries; \"you're so smart\" grows one who fears being wrong. The words are small; the difference compounds for years."},{"type":"h2","text":"Protect the wind-down"},{"type":"p","text":"The hour before bed sets the tone for the night and the morning after. Dimmer lights, lower voices, and screens away make sleep arrive on its own instead of by force."},{"type":"h2","text":"One-on-one beats the group"},{"type":"p","text":"Even a quick errand run solo with one child fills a cup that group time can't reach. Rot