 to fill every gap teaches kids to generate their own fun — a skill worth more than any enrichment class."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, write down the plan so it survives a chaotic day. Next, decide the one default that removes a daily decision. Then, pick the single piece of the gentle end to thumb-sucking that causes the most friction this week."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"If keeping the gentle end to thumb-sucking on track sounds exhausting, that's the point where Bubaly quietly helps. It's the AI operating system for family life — it remembers, reminds, and coordinates so you don't have to carry it all alone. See how at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"Give it a couple of weeks before you judge it. Habits feel awkward right up until the moment they feel obvious."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('the-honest-guide-to-teaching-kids-to-keep-promises', 'The Honest Guide to Teaching Kids to Keep Promises', 'Most families don''t struggle with teaching kids to keep promises because they''re doing it wrong — they struggle because nobody handed them a system. Below is a calm, practical way ', 'Daniel Okafor', '2025-01-13', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#teachingkids','#parentinghacks','#organizedhome']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/child,parent,together?lock=1074', 'A photo related to teaching kids to keep promises.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"Most families don't struggle with teaching kids to keep promises because they're doing it wrong — they struggle because nobody handed them a system."},{"type":"p","text":"Below is a calm, practical way to think about it — not a rigid program, just a handful of ideas you can borrow tonight and adjust for your own family."},{"type":"h2","text":"Fewer, firmer boundaries"},{"type":"p","text":"Households run smoother with a handful of non-negotiables held kindly than with fifty rules enforced at random. Decide the few that matter, say them once, and let natural consequences do the teaching."},{"type":"h2","text":"Consistency over intensity"},{"type":"p","text":"A quiet rule kept every day outperforms a dramatic one kept once. Kids are scientists; they run experiments on our boundaries, and steadiness gives them the answer faster than volume."},{"type":"h2","text":"Let them be bored"},{"type":"p","text":"Boredom is the doorway to imagination. Resisting the urge to fill every gap teaches kids to generate their own fun — a skill worth more than any enrichment class."},{"type":"h2","text":"Catch them being good"},{"type":"p","text":"Attention is fertilizer; whatever you water grows. Narrate the small wins — \"you put your shoes away without me asking\" — and you'll see more of them."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"Fir