('a-simple-system-for-a-command-center-for-a-small-space', 'A Simple System for A Command Center for a Small Space', 'Ask ten parents about a command center for a small space and you''ll get ten answers, forty opinions, and one shared sigh. We''ll walk through what actually moves the needle, what to', 'Marcus Bell', '2025-03-17', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#familyorganization','#commandcenter','#raisingkids','#organizedhome']::text[], 'Organization', false, '#3b82f6', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/organized,home,cleaning?lock=1131', 'A photo related to a command center for a small space.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"Ask ten parents about a command center for a small space and you'll get ten answers, forty opinions, and one shared sigh."},{"type":"p","text":"We'll walk through what actually moves the needle, what to skip, and how to make it feel less like a chore and more like a shared routine."},{"type":"h2","text":"Plan the week backward"},{"type":"p","text":"Start from the fixed points — practices, appointments, work — and slot the flexible stuff around them. Planning backward from what can't move keeps the week honest."},{"type":"h2","text":"Capture, then organize"},{"type":"p","text":"The brain is for having ideas, not holding them. A single trusted inbox — one notebook, one app — where everything lands first means nothing slips through the cracks at 11pm."},{"type":"h2","text":"One in, one out"},{"type":"p","text":"The fastest way to keep clutter from creeping back is a quiet trade: a new toy in, an old one out. It keeps volume flat without a single big purge."},{"type":"h2","text":"Launch pad by the door"},{"type":"p","text":"A single spot where everything that leaves the house lives — bags, shoes, water bottles, signed forms — turns frantic mornings into a grab-and-go. What's not on the launch pad doesn't make it out."},{"type":"h2","text":"Label like you'll forget"},{"type":"p","text":"Because you will, and so will everyone else. Clear labels turn a shared closet from a mystery into a system anyone in the family can maintain without you."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, give it two weeks before you judge whether it's working. Next, put it somewhere the whole family can see it. Then, agree on who owns it so it isn't quietly one person's job."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"This is exactly the kind of invisible work Bubaly was built for. Instead of holding a command center for a small space in your head, you hand it to your family's shared system — Bubaly turns plans into real calendar events, reminders, lists, and gentle nudges, so the whole household stays in sync. Explore it at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"You'll still have messy days. That's fine. A good routine bends without breaking."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('rethinking-managing-the-school-year-transition-for-the-modern-family', 'Rethinking Managing the School-year Transition for the Modern Family', 'There''s a version of managing the school-year transition that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. We''ll walk through what actually moves the needle, what to', 'The Bubaly Team', '2025-04-08', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#familyorganization','#managingschoolyear','#familyfirst','#familytime']::text[], 'Organization', false, '#3b82f6', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/school,children,classroom?lock=1132', 'A photo related to managing the school-year transition.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"There's a version of managing the school-year transition that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. This is about getting to the second one."},{"type":"p","text":"We'll walk through what actually moves the needle, what to skip, and how to make it feel less like a chore and more like a shared routine."},{"type":"h2","text":"Reduce the surfaces"},{"type":"p","text":"Every flat surface is a magnet for clutter. Fewer open shelves and more closed storage means less visual noise and far less to tidy."},{"type":"h2","text":"One in, one out"},{"type":"p","text":"The fastest way to keep clutter from creeping back is a quiet trade: a new toy in, an old one out. It keeps volume flat without a single big purge."},{"type":"h2","text":"Batch the boring stuff"},{"type":"p","text":"Errands, emails, and forms are cheaper in bulk. Grouping the small administrative tasks into one window beats letting them interrupt your day fifteen times."},{"type":"h2","text":"A home for everything"},{"type":"p","text":"Clutter is mostly deferred decisions. When keys, backpacks, and permission slips each have one obvious home, tidying stops being a project and becomes a reflex. Start managing the school-year transition by deciding where things live."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"