('everything-families-get-wrong-about-managing-the-science-project-supply-run', 'Everything Families Get Wrong About Managing the Science-project Supply Run', 'Managing the Science-project Supply Run is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore. Below is a calm, practica', 'Hannah Cho', '2026-01-10', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#schoollife','#managingscienceproject','#momlife','#dadlife']::text[], 'School & Activities', false, '#10b981', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/homework,study,desk?lock=1201', 'A photo related to managing the science-project supply run.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"Managing the Science-project Supply Run is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore."},{"type":"p","text":"Below is a calm, practical way to think about it — not a rigid program, just a handful of ideas you can borrow tonight and adjust for your own family."},{"type":"h2","text":"Protect the sleep, guard the mornings"},{"type":"p","text":"Nearly every school-morning battle traces back to bedtime the night before. Win the evening and the morning mostly wins itself."},{"type":"h2","text":"Pack the night before"},{"type":"p","text":"Mornings are for leaving, not deciding. Bags packed, clothes out, lunches half-made the night before turns the school run from a sprint into a walk."},{"type":"h2","text":"Talk to the teacher early"},{"type":"p","text":"A friendly note in week two beats a tense meeting in month three. Teachers are allies; the earlier you're on the same page, the smaller every problem stays."},{"type":"h2","text":"Right-size the activities"},{"type":"p","text":"A packed schedule looks impressive and feels awful. One or two activities a child genuinely loves beats five they tolerate — and it gives the whole family its evenings back."},{"type":"h2","text":"Don't do the homework"},{"type":"p","text":"The goal is a capable kid, not a finished worksheet. \"Quiz me,\" \"explain it back,\" and \"where are you stuck?\" build skills; taking over builds dependence."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, decide the one default that removes a daily decision. Next, put it somewhere the whole family can see it. Then, give it two weeks before you judge whether it's working."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"Want managing the science-project supply run to run itself? That's what Bubaly does — one calm home for your family's schedule, tasks, meals, and money, with AI that stays a step ahead. Start at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"None of this has to be perfect. Pick one idea, try it for a week, and keep only what earns its place."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('everything-families-get-wrong-about-helping-kids-take-good-notes', 'Everything Families Get Wrong About Helping Kids Take Good Notes', 'The secret to helping kids take good notes isn''t more willpower. We''ll walk through what actually moves the needle, what to skip, and how to make it feel less like a chore and more', 'Elena Rossi', '2026-01-24', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#schoollife','#helpingkids','#organizedhome','#familytime']::text[], 'School & Activities', false, '#10b981', 'https://loremflickr.com/1600/900/homework,study,desk?lock=1202', 'A photo related to helping kids take good notes.', 'LoremFlickr (CC)', $json$[{"type":"p","text":"The secret to helping kids take good notes isn't more willpower. It's less friction — fewer decisions, clearer defaults, and a plan everyone can see."},{"type":"p","text":"We'll walk through what actually moves the needle, what to skip, and how to make it feel less like a chore and more like a shared routine."},{"type":"h2","text":"A visible countdown to due dates"},{"type":"p","text":"Big projects sink kids because \"next week\" isn't real to them. A visible countdown broken into small steps turns a scary deadline into a series of easy Tuesdays."},{"type":"h2","text":"Right-size the activities"},{"type":"p","text":"A packed schedule looks impressive and feels awful. One or two activities a child genuinely loves beats five they tolerate — and it gives the whole family its evenings back."},{"type":"h2","text":"Build a launch-and-land routine"},{"type":"p","text":"The first ten minutes home and the last ten before bed set the tone for school. Predictable bookends make the chaotic middle much easier to steer."},{"type":"h2","text":"Read the newsletter for two facts"},{"type":"p","text":"School emails are warm, long, and mostly context. Skim for the two things that need an action — a date, a form — and turn each into a calendar event before you close the tab."},{"type":"h2","text":"Pack the night before"},{"type":"p","tex